        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buying a USB-C charger comes down to one number and one caveat. The number is the single-port wattage, which should match the charger your laptop came with. The caveat is that the big figure on a multi-port block is shared. Get those right, add a cable rated for the job, and almost any reputable GaN charger will serve you well.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Watts Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best USB-C charger is the smallest one that gives your most demanding device its full rated power. That makes the first job simple: look at what you are charging. The wattage printed on your laptop's original charger is the most it will ever ask for. Phones and tablets do not print it as clearly, but their spec pages list a maximum charging rate, and that is the number to match.
          </p>

          <p>
            Phones sit in a narrow band. Recent iPhones fast-charge from a 20-watt adapter and can briefly accept a bit more, while some Android flagships charge at 25, 45 or more watts if the charger supports the right protocol. Tablets typically want 20 to 45 watts. A 20-watt charging block is plenty for a phone on a nightstand; a 30-to-45-watt block makes sense if you also charge a tablet or want the fastest top-up for a Samsung.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laptops are where the numbers climb. A MacBook Air, a Chromebook or a 13-inch Windows ultrabook typically ships with a 30 to 65 watt charger, which is why 65W chargers are such a popular category. A 14-inch performance laptop usually wants 65 to 100 watts. A 16-inch MacBook Pro ships with a 140-watt adapter, and gaming laptops with big graphics chips often use barrel-plug bricks of 180 watts or more that no USB-C charger replaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            A smaller charger still works with a bigger laptop, it just charges more slowly, and under heavy use the battery may drain slowly even while plugged in. A larger charger is always safe, because USB-C devices only draw what they negotiate. That is why a 65W charger for a phone is fine and why a 100W charger is the most flexible single block for a mixed household. If you want to charge several devices from one spot on a desk, a <a href="/best-charging-station/">multi-device charging station</a> is the next step up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the laptop, then add headroom for sharing.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy a charger whose single-port maximum equals your laptop's original charger, then add 20 to 35 watts of total headroom if you plan to charge a phone from the same block at the same time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="pd-pps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. USB Power Delivery and PPS, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            USB Power Delivery, almost always shortened to PD, is the standard that makes one charger work with a phone, a tablet and a laptop. When you plug in, the charger and device negotiate over the cable: the device says what it can accept, and the charger offers fixed voltage steps such as 5, 9, 15 and 20 volts. A phone might settle on 9 volts at around 2 amps, a laptop on 20 volts at 3.25 amps for 65 watts. All of this happens automatically and is why a good USB-C charger is genuinely universal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standard PD tops out at 20 volts and 5 amps, which is 100 watts. The PD 3.1 revision added the extended power range, with higher voltage steps of 28, 36 and 48 volts, raising the ceiling to 240 watts. That is how a 140-watt charger feeds a 16-inch MacBook Pro over USB-C. Extended power only works if the charger, the cable and the device all support it; otherwise everything falls back to standard 100-watt PD.
          </p>

          <p>
            PPS, the programmable power supply, is an optional part of PD that lets the device ask for voltage in very fine steps instead of fixed ones. That allows a phone to move some of the charging work out of its own circuitry and into the charger, which runs cooler and faster. Samsung's Super Fast Charging at 25 and 45 watts relies on PPS, and some other Android phones use it too. iPhones do not need PPS for their fastest wired charging.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, PPS support is the one spec Android owners should actively check. A charger can say 65 watts and still charge a Galaxy phone at a slower standard rate if it lacks PPS, or if its PPS range does not include what the phone wants. Good listings state PPS support on the USB-C ports; when a listing is vague about it, assume it is not there. Older proprietary protocols such as Qualcomm Quick Charge still exist on some chargers' USB-A ports but matter less every year.
          </p>
        </div>

