        <div id="gan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What GaN Actually Buys You
          </h2>

          <p>
            GaN stands for gallium nitride, a semiconductor material that has largely replaced silicon in good chargers. GaN transistors switch faster and waste less energy as heat than silicon ones, which lets designers use smaller components and pack more power into a smaller block. That is the entire benefit, and it is a real one: a modern GaN 65W charger is often not much larger than an old 20-watt phone brick, and a GaN 100W block is a fraction of the size of a typical 100-watt laptop charger from a few years ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            What GaN does not do is charge your devices faster. Speed is set by the wattage and the protocols the charger supports, not by the material inside it. A silicon 65-watt charger and a GaN 65-watt charger deliver the same 65 watts. The GaN version is smaller and lighter and often runs a little cooler, which matters in a bag and in a crowded outlet, but it will not make a phone charge any quicker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because GaN is now the norm for name-brand laptop-class chargers, it has become a marketing label as much as a feature. Some listings put GaN in large type on chargers that are otherwise unremarkable, or use generation numbers that mean little across brands. Treat GaN as a baseline expectation for a 65-watt or larger block, not as a reason to pay more on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size benefit has one practical downside: heat density. A small block delivering 100 watts has less surface to shed heat, so it will feel warm under a sustained laptop load. That is normal. A charger that is too hot to hold, smells, or makes crackling noises is not, and should be unplugged and replaced. Owners commonly report that the better-known GaN brands stay warm rather than hot, which is one of the reasons to buy from them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sharing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Power Sharing Across Ports
          </h2>

          <p>
            Multi-port USB-C chargers advertise their total, and that total is almost never available on every port at once. A 65-watt three-port charger can give 65 watts to one USB-C device, but connect a phone to the second USB-C port and it will typically split into something like 45 and 20 watts. Add something to the USB-A port and the split changes again. The total stays roughly the same; it is just divided.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every time a device is connected or removed, most chargers briefly cut power to all ports while they renegotiate. Your laptop may flash its charging indicator, and a phone may show a momentary disconnect. That is expected behavior, not a fault. It is also why a multi-port charger is a poor choice for anything that must never lose power for a second, such as an external drive mid-backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            USB-A ports deserve a specific warning. On many chargers, using the USB-A port reduces the output of the USB-C ports more than you would expect, and the USB-A port itself is slow. If you have a choice, use USB-C cables for everything and leave the USB-A port for an old pair of earbuds or a flashlight. If you own nothing that needs USB-A, a two-port USB-C charger like the Belkin is a cleaner fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is to size the charger for the combination you actually use. If a laptop and phone charge together every evening, buy a charger whose sharing table still gives the laptop its full rating with the phone connected. A 100-watt block generally gives a 65-watt laptop its full rate alongside a phone, while a 65-watt block gives that laptop less. For a desk with four or more devices, a <a href="/best-charging-station/">charging station</a> with a higher total is a better fit than a wall block.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cables: 60W, 100W and 240W E-Marked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A USB-C charger is only half of the system. The cable decides how much power can actually flow, and it is the most common reason a good charger seems slow. USB-C cables come in three practical power classes, and they look nearly identical, so it is worth knowing which ones you own before blaming the charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            A basic USB-C cable without an electronic marker chip is limited to 3 amps. At 20 volts that is 60 watts, which is fine for phones, tablets and thin laptops on a 60- or 65-watt charger, though a 65-watt laptop may top out just under its full rate. Most cables that come in phone boxes are in this class, and many are also limited to slow USB 2.0 data speeds, which does not affect charging.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 100-watt cable carries an e-marker chip that tells the charger it can safely handle 5 amps. You need one to get more than 60 watts out of a 100-watt charger. A 240-watt cable is rated for the extended power range and is required for anything above 100 watts, such as the Apple 140W on a 16-inch MacBook Pro. Good cables say their rating on the packaging, and many printed or molded markings now show it on the connector.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy cables from the same reputable brands as chargers. A cheap, unmarked cable that claims 100 watts may not have a real e-marker, and a damaged cable is a heat and fire risk regardless of its rating. Replace any cable with exposed wire, a loose connector, or a plug that gets hot. For laptop-class charging, one good 240-watt cable is a small cost that removes a whole category of slow-charging problems and lets the cable follow you to a bigger charger later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety Certifications and Counterfeits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wall charger sits between household current and a lithium battery you keep next to your head at night, so it is not a place to save the last few dollars. Look for a listing mark from a nationally recognized testing laboratory, such as UL, ETL or CSA, on the charger or its documentation, along with the FCC mark. Major brands carry these as standard. Unbranded chargers with no marks and a too-good price are where most charger fires and failures come from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Counterfeits are a specific problem with Apple chargers and, increasingly, popular Anker models. A fake can look nearly identical from the outside while skipping the insulation, fuses and protection circuits inside. The safest approach is to buy from the brand's own store, a major retailer, or a marketplace listing that is sold and shipped by the brand or the retailer itself. A charger that is unusually light, has blurry printing, or feels loose in the outlet is worth returning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good chargers include protection against over-voltage, over-current, short circuits and over-temperature, and most brands list these. Use them sensibly anyway. Keep chargers out of bedding and off couches, do not charge devices under a pillow, and unplug any charger that gets hot, buzzes, smells or has a discolored plug. Do not use a charger with a cracked case or bent prongs, and do not force a charger into a loose or damaged outlet; have a licensed electrician replace worn receptacles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surges are a separate issue. A charger's internal protection is not a surge protector, and lightning or utility switching can still damage it and what is connected. For a home office, plugging chargers into a quality surge-protected strip is sensible, and a <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector</a> installed at the panel by a licensed electrician protects every circuit at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. USB-C Chargers and Power Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            A USB-C charger is useless in an outage unless it has somewhere to get power, but the devices it charges are the ones you most want running. The practical plan is to know which battery will stand in for the wall. For a phone or two, a power bank is enough. For laptops and a household of phones over several days, a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a> is the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most current power stations have their own USB-C PD ports, often rated at 60 to 100 watts or more. Use those directly when you can. Plugging a wall charger into the power station's AC outlet works fine, but it means running the station's inverter to make AC, then converting it straight back to DC in the charger, and the losses add up over days. The wall charger route is still useful when you need more USB-C ports than the station has.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank</a>, check that its USB-C output matches your laptop the same way you would for a wall charger. A <a href="/best-battery-pack-with-ac-outlet/">battery pack with an AC outlet</a> can run a small multi-port charger for several phones. In a car, a USB-C car charger with PD lets you top up phones while the engine runs, but never run a car or generator in a closed garage to charge devices; carbon monoxide builds up quickly and kills.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generators deserve the same caution. A portable generator belongs outdoors, far from doors, windows and vents, with a working CO alarm inside the house. Plug chargers into the generator with a properly rated outdoor extension cord, not into a wall outlet that is being fed by the generator through a homemade cable, which is backfeeding and is both illegal and deadly. A home connection needs a transfer switch or interlock installed by a licensed electrician under permit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which USB-C Charger to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one charging block that handles a laptop and a phone, buy the <a href="#anker-prime-100w">Anker Prime 100W</a>. It covers almost every USB-C laptop at full speed on its own and still charges a phone and earbuds alongside. If you need four ports, or the Prime is out of budget, the <a href="#ugreen-nexode-100w">UGREEN Nexode 100W</a> does the same job with an extra port for less money in a larger block.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your laptop is a MacBook Air, a Chromebook or another thin machine that shipped with a 30 to 65 watt charger, a 65-watt charger is all you need. The <a href="#anker-735-nano-ii">Anker 735</a> and the <a href="#ugreen-nexode-65w">UGREEN Nexode 65W</a> are close enough that price should decide it. Choose the <a href="#belkin-boostcharge-pro-65w">Belkin BoostCharge Pro 65W</a> if you want USB-C only or need to buy one in a store today.
          </p>

          <p>
            For phones alone, buy the <a href="#anker-nano-20w">Anker Nano 20W</a>, ideally several. The <a href="#apple-20w-usb-c">Apple 20W</a> is a fine alternative if you want Apple's own adapter and buy it from a trustworthy seller, but it is not better. Samsung owners who want the fastest wired charging should step up to a 45-watt charger with confirmed PPS support instead of either 20-watt brick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only buy the <a href="#apple-140w-usb-c">Apple 140W</a> if you own a 16-inch MacBook Pro and want a spare that matches the one in the box, and pair it with a 240-watt cable. Whatever you choose, buy a cable rated for the wattage, buy from a seller that cannot pass off a counterfeit, and keep a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">power station</a> or power bank charged so your charger has something to plug into when the power goes out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the total instead of the single-port rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 100-watt three-port charger gives 100 watts to one device only when nothing else is plugged in. Add a phone and the laptop port drops. Match the single-port maximum, with your usual combination connected, to the wattage of the charger your laptop shipped with, and treat the total as a measure of capacity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the charger for a slow cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary USB-C cables without an e-marker cap out at 60 watts, and many phone-box cables are exactly that. A 100-watt charger on one of them cannot deliver more. Use a 5-amp e-marked cable for 100 watts and a 240-watt cable for anything above, from a reputable brand that prints the rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring PPS on a Samsung phone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Samsung's fastest wired charging uses PPS. A big-watt charger without PPS, or with a PPS range that does not match, will charge a Galaxy at a slower standard rate. If your phone advertises Super Fast Charging, confirm the charger lists PPS on its USB-C ports before buying for that reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cheap Apple lookalike</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Counterfeit Apple and Anker chargers are common in marketplace listings and can skip the insulation and protection circuits that make a charger safe. Buy from the brand, a major retailer, or a listing shipped and sold by one of them. Return anything unusually light, poorly printed or loose in the outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging chargers into a backfed outlet in an outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Feeding a generator or power station into a wall outlet with a double-ended cable to run chargers is backfeeding. It can electrocute utility workers and start fires. Plug chargers directly into the generator or power station, keep generators outdoors far from openings, and have a licensed electrician install a transfer switch for home connections.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best USB-C charger for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one block that charges a laptop and a phone, the Anker Prime 100W is the strongest all-rounder, with the UGREEN Nexode 100W as a cheaper four-port alternative. If you only have a thin laptop, a 65-watt charger such as the Anker 735 or UGREEN Nexode 65W is enough. For phones alone, a 20-watt brick like the Anker Nano is all you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 65W charger safe for my phone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. USB-C Power Delivery chargers only supply what the device asks for, so a phone on a 65-watt charger negotiates its own rate, usually 20 to 45 watts, and the charger provides no more. The same applies to earbuds and watches. A higher-watt charger is never forced into a device; the device is always in control of what it draws.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does GaN mean on a charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                GaN stands for gallium nitride, a semiconductor material that wastes less energy as heat than silicon. It lets manufacturers build smaller, lighter chargers for the same wattage. It does not make devices charge faster; charging speed comes from wattage and protocol support. For any charger of 65 watts or more, GaN is now a baseline expectation rather than a premium feature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a special cable for a 100W charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To get more than 60 watts, yes. Basic USB-C cables are limited to 3 amps, or 60 watts at 20 volts. For 100 watts you need a 5-amp e-marked cable, and anything above 100 watts, such as 140-watt MacBook Pro charging, requires a cable rated for 240 watts. Check the rating printed on the packaging or connector.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best charging block for an iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 20-watt USB-C charger is the sweet spot for wired iPhone charging. Apple says its 20W adapter can bring a compatible iPhone to about half charge in around 30 minutes. The Anker Nano 20W does the same job in a smaller body for less. A bigger charger will not hurt the phone, and is worth it if you also charge an iPad or MacBook.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a USB-C charger power a laptop that came with a barrel plug?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Laptops with round barrel chargers do not accept USB-C power unless they also have a USB-C port that supports charging, which the manufacturer will list. Many gaming laptops have both, but USB-C charging often tops out at 100 watts and may only slow the drain under heavy use. Check the laptop's spec sheet before buying a charger for it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy Anker, UGREEN, Belkin or Apple?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                All four make good chargers when you buy the genuine article. Anker and UGREEN tend to offer the most ports and wattage for the money, Belkin is easy to find in stores, and Apple's adapters are simple, reliable and more expensive. Choose by the wattage and port count you need, then buy whichever reputable model is cheapest from a trustworthy seller.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

